<section id="start-with-the-document-you-already-reopen">
<span class="ls-essay-section-number">07</span>
<h2>FOLD: turn one intention into a practice</h2>
<p>The bridge between a large ambition and a useful document is a small practice. For Living Documents, I use an adaptation of FOLD: Frame the outcome, Organize the routine, Leave a receipt, Develop the practice.</p>
<p>
<strong>Frame the outcome.</strong> Choose a page you already reopen. Write down what would make returning easier: a weekly view of campaign results, a research brief with current sources, or a budget whose chart follows new data. A clear outcome helps you recognize a useful result and reject a distracting one.</p>
<p>
<strong>Organize the routine.</strong> Name the sources, the permitted changes, and the rhythm. A researcher may watch selected public pages. A creator may work from their own exports. A small team may begin with files it already exchanges. Flow’s editors let you define the recurring work and inspect its definitions. Begin with a task small enough to understand completely.</p>
<p>
<strong>Leave a receipt.</strong> Run a cycle and inspect what happened. Read the changed part alongside its sources and checks. Notice what failed and what stayed unchanged. Keep or revert the result. A receipt helps the future you understand the choice the present you made.</p>
<p>
<strong>Develop the practice.</strong> Return for a second cycle. Correct the definition that was too broad. Remove the update that added noise. Keep the comparison that helped you see something. A method becomes valuable through the care accumulated in it.</p>
<p>Flow includes seven Living Document examples as starting points. Their sources and definitions are available to explore and adapt. None can choose your purpose for you. FOLD is a way to make that purpose durable enough to test, then carry what you learn into the next cycle.</p>
</section>
